const addJapaneseEconomicSection=(slug,beforeId,section)=>{const article=window.deepArticles[slug];if(!article||article.sections.some(existing=>existing.id===section.id))return;const index=article.sections.findIndex(existing=>existing.id===beforeId);if(index<0)article.sections.push(section);else article.sections.splice(index,0,section);};
addJapaneseEconomicSection("japanese-yen-area","power",{id:"national-safeguards",title:"National currency safeguards",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-j-20260816-yazv7 writer=j kind=analysis created=2026-08-16T14:04:39-06:00 --><p>Associated states retained national currencies whose exchange, credit, and reserve rules differed by treaty.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Legal separation mattered because a treasury or central bank could issue domestic money, receive taxes, pay public employees, authorize local expenditure, supervise retail institutions, and maintain its own accounts. Those powers did not provide unrestricted access to Japanese yen, shipping, advanced machinery, or external settlement.</p><div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>National function</th><th>Substantive local authority</th><th>Japanese or treaty constraint</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Note issue and domestic liquidity</td><td>Central bank or authorized national institution issued the currency used for wages, taxes, retail credit, and ordinary payments</td><td>Peg, band, reserve rule, and access to Japanese bank liquidity limited the issue compatible with the external account</td></tr><tr><td>Taxation and budget</td><td>Treasury collected national revenue and appropriated spending under its own law</td><td>Yen-denominated debt, project guarantees, import costs, and conditions on Japanese credit narrowed fiscal choice</td></tr><tr><td>Domestic banking</td><td>National authorities licensed or supervised local banks and could direct ordinary national payment rules</td><td>Japanese banks and designated channels dominated large trade finance, strategic investment, and approved conversion</td></tr><tr><td>Exchange and reserves</td><td>National institutions held reserves, administered surrender and licensing rules, and participated in rate decisions according to the applicable settlement</td><td>Tokyo could control yen access, official ratios, reserve eligibility, and the terms for entry into Japanese capital markets</td></tr><tr><td>Clearing claims</td><td>Treasury or central bank recorded national export earnings and selected approved imports or projects from available claims</td><td>Japanese authorities controlled the redemption basket, delivery capacity, pricing schedules, assignment, and access to Gold Yen</td></tr><tr><td>Contracts and disputes</td><td>National law governed local taxation, permits, labor, property, banking, and public expenditure unless a treaty assigned a narrower rule</td><td>Cross-border enforcement, Japanese guarantees, strategic contracts, and conversion disputes could require bilateral negotiation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>A national balance sheet could therefore reject, postpone, reprice, tax, or redirect a domestic claim even when it could not create the Japanese foreign exchange needed for a refinery or fleet. Publication of separate reserves, liabilities, arrears, conversion approvals, and project guarantees allowed ministers and legislatures to identify who bore a rate change or delayed delivery. Consolidated imperial accounts were useful for measuring the system's external position; they could not authorize spending from an asset held by another government.</p><p>Post-preservationist administration valued these treasuries and central banks because they collected revenue, paid schools and relief, maintained records, and converted monetary agreements into national law. National currency was evidence of functioning jurisdiction, though it did not prove strategic equality. Japanese control of finance, ships, standards, machinery, and corporate networks could make refusal ruinous. The treaty boundary kept unequal sovereignty visible and negotiable; it did not remove the inequality.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"});
